<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Hacker 2.0 | Declassification: The Stripe/H1 Case Study</title>
    <style>
        :root { --accent: #ff4444; --bg: #0a0a0a; --text: #e0e0e0; --code-bg: #1a1a1a; }
        body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0; padding: 20px; }
        .container { max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; border: 1px solid #333; padding: 40px; background: rgba(20, 20, 20, 0.95); }
        h1, h2, h3 { color: var(--accent); text-transform: uppercase; border-bottom: 1px solid #333; padding-bottom: 10px; }
        .timeline-tree { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 30px 0; border: 1px dashed #444; padding: 20px; background: #111; }
        .timeline-tree li { margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 20px; position: relative; font-size: 0.95rem; }
        .timeline-tree li::before { content: '•'; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--accent); }
        .timeline-tree b { color: #fff; }
        .evidence-section { margin-top: 50px; }
        .evidence-item { margin-bottom: 60px; }
        .evidence-text { background: var(--code-bg); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); padding: 20px; margin: 15px 0; }
        img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid #444; margin-top: 15px; display: block; box-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.5); }
        .status-badge { display: inline-block; padding: 5px 15px; background: var(--accent); color: #000; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 20px; }
        .csv-data { font-size: 0.8rem; background: #000; padding: 15px; border: 1px solid #333; overflow-x: auto; white-space: pre; color: #00ff41; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="container">
    <div class="status-badge">CASE REPORT: #STRP-25K-USD</div>
    <h1>The Anatomy of a Systemic Rip-Off</h1>
    
    <h3>Summary Timeline</h3>
    <ul class="timeline-tree">
        <li><b>[MAR 16]</b> Meesho report marked N/A - No H1 assistance - Reputation loss (-5) - Lockout initiated.</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 19 12:49]</b> Urgent request to H1 Support to allow Critical Stripe report - <b>DENIED.</b></li>
        <li><b>[MAR 20 22:50]</b> Bypass forced via direct intervention from Stripe Security on X (Twitter).</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 20 23:06]</b> CVSS 10.0 Report submitted to Stripe via HackerOne.</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 21 08:03]</b> Stripe Support confirms bypass successful; thanks researcher for disclosure.</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 21 22:56]</b> <b>CONFIRMED FIX:</b> Stripe Support (Divine) confirms bug is resolved.</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 23 16:14]</b> HackerOne triager attempts to mark report as Duplicate of "Informative."</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 23]</b> Unrelated report (ID: 3612501) appears in Stripe Hacktivity timeline.</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 25 15:49]</b> Stripe Security claims vulnerability is "Intended Behavior."</li>
        <li><b>[MAR 25 17:12]</b> <b>REBUTTAL:</b> Proof discovered that redirect_uri was restricted post-disclosure.</li>
        <li><b>[APR 08]</b> <b>INTERNAL RECEIPT:</b> Stripe Legal reviews formal demand via Amazon/Google proxies.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="evidence-section">
        <h2>01. The Administrative Blockade</h2>
        <div class="evidence-item">
            <p>HackerOne's automated systems and support staff actively prioritized reputation points over security, refusing to bypass a manual lockout even for a CVSS 10.0 threat.</p>
            <div class="evidence-text">"Trial reports are limited... they cannot be retrieved or reset manually." - H1 Support</div>
            <img src="H1-deny-submitting-report1.png" alt="H1 Refusal to Allow Report">
            <img src="1Broken-ticket-system.png" alt="Broken Ticket Loop">
        </div>

        <h2>02. The Intervention</h2>
        <div class="evidence-item">
            <p>Direct communication with Stripe Security was required to bypass the platform's incompetence.</p>
            <img src="notified-stripe-support-onX.png" alt="Stripe Support on X">
            <img src="email-to-allow-reporting.png" alt="Bypass Confirmation">
        </div>

        <h2>03. The Critical Submission (CVSS 10.0)</h2>
        <div class="evidence-item">
            <p>The report detailed a full platform takeover via unauthenticated dynamic client registration.</p>
            <img src="report.png" alt="Original Report Overview">
        </div>

        <h2>04. The "Smoking Gun" (March 21st Fix)</h2>
        <div class="evidence-item">
            <p>Within 24 hours, Stripe Support confirmed the bug was resolved. This admission was later contradicted by Security teams to avoid payment.</p>
            <img src="1stripe-support-email-with-dates-times.png" alt="Stripe Support Fix Confirmation">
        </div>

        <h2>05. The Deception (Shadow-Patching)</h2>
        <div class="evidence-item">
            <p>On March 25th, Stripe claimed the behavior was "intended," yet their own system logs prove they hardened the redirect logic immediately following the report.</p>
            <img src="restricted-redirect_uri.png" alt="Post-Report Hardening Proof">
            <img src="1marked-as-duplicate-told-intended.png" alt="H1 Duplicate Marking">
        </div>

        <h2>06. Hacktivity Discrepancies</h2>
        <div class="evidence-item">
            <p>Suspicious timing of other "Informative" reports appearing in the timeline while this CVSS 10.0 was being devalued.</p>
            <img src="timeline-other-report-likely-fake.png" alt="Hacktivity Timeline Analysis">
        </div>

        <h2>07. Forensic Proof of Receipt</h2>
        <div class="evidence-item">
            <p>Data exported from the formal legal demand sent to Stripe. Internal network hits confirm the demand was processed by Stripe's infrastructure (FrontApp/Amazon/Google proxies) despite their public silence.</p>
            <div class="csv-data">
Timestamp, Source, Geo Info, User Agent
2026-04-08 04:28:18, 120.19.3.85, Sydney AU (Vodafone), Firefox 140.0
2026-04-08 16:55:04, 52.30.32.36, Amazon.com Inc / FrontApp ImageProxy
2026-04-08 16:56:12, 66.249.84.99, Google LLC Proxy (Oregon, US)
            </div>
            <p>The presence of <b>FrontApp</b> and <b>Amazon/Google</b> proxies in the logs is a fingerprint of corporate email triage systems opening the demand.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 60px; opacity: 0.6;">
        <a href="index.html" style="color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none;">[ EXIT DECLASSIFICATION ]</a>
    </p>
</div>

</body>
</html>
